; STORE-SHELL §4 (the names plane, as amended by W3-14) — F-39 CLOSED.
;
; THE DEFECT THIS SCRIPT USED TO BE. This is `r2-11-name-case-collision.script` from the
; wave-2 refuter battery, where it was an S1 FAILURE: the model's name plane is keyed by an
; exact Lean `String`, the disk plane was one file per name, and APFS and NTFS both fold
; case. Two model bindings became one file. The receipt:
;
;     FAIL A1-name-case.script
;          DIVERGENCE at line 10:
;           model: 5 | ok 27d77d3b…a38f
;           disk : 5 | ok bf279c82…a572e
;
; Step 5 is `(name-get "Widget")`. The model answered with step 1's address, the disk with
; step 2's, and BOTH EXITED 0 — a silent wrong answer, which is what puts it in the S1
; tier. On disk one file survived, keeping the FIRST binding's spelling and the SECOND
; binding's content.
;
; WHY IT PASSES NOW. W3-14 ruled that the model's `String` keys are authoritative and the
; filename stops being the key: a name lives at `names/<lowercase hex of its UTF-8 bytes>`.
; `Widget` is `576964676574` and `widget` is `776964676574` — two filenames that differ in
; their FIRST BYTE, on a plane whose alphabet is `[0-9a-f]`. No filesystem folds those
; together. The model side did not move at all; that is the point of the ruling.
;
; This is the class-closure observable: the script is committed in the position where it
; used to fail, and its passing IS the amendment record (PROCEDURE §5 ratchet).

(schema-put (prim int))
(schema-put (prim str))

; two distinct objects, so a plane answering with the wrong one is visible rather than
; accidentally right
(assert-differ @1 @2)

(name-set "Widget" @1)
(name-set "widget" @2)

; the divergence's exact site. Each name resolves to ITS OWN address, on both planes.
(name-get "Widget")
(name-get "widget")

; TWO bindings, not one: `names=2` is the count that used to be 1 on disk and 2 in the
; model while both sides exited 0
(check)
(assert-code prev 0)

; and the listing shows both, decoded — the same two keys the model holds
(names)

; case is not the only host relation that could have collapsed the pair. Rebinding one
; must leave the other alone, which a one-file-for-both plane could never manage.
(name-set "Widget" @2)
(name-get "Widget")
(name-get "widget")
(names)
(check)
(assert-code prev 0)

; and the objects plane never noticed any of it (M16, L-names-inert)
(get @1)
(resolve @2)
